#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b4648b-d247-5f92-9d2b-f244cac28ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259878} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1a741e-d70d-5584-8f47-17f6fe12b753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab7c77-806c-599b-bff5-2271fa35fc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260640} {:answer/id #uuid "825a056c-6bef-533e-873b-b5c171a4706c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261008} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84226c-f819-56cf-82e3-e3cbf03ace66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3d48da-ade1-5236-99b3-6c5eeeefbc63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8d84a8f-b961-5115-a51c-b62b1cb780d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127190" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009f40ac-91df-5ff9-993b-186929aec51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259876} {:answer/id #uuid "8d61b731-ab18-54f1-93e0-e5ca68013f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261112} {:answer/id #uuid "9060bbb8-05be-51ce-a85b-98fdecf11014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261131} {:answer/id #uuid "e48093a6-59c4-5f9e-8abb-72c28b727a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb8b743-8dc5-5437-83b1-ac7f99241c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261131} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0f2493-5bc7-580a-b18f-50e22975871f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "622ab5d8-9ae6-5da5-96af-e733a10932b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127194" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d550e73-f433-5554-9114-a2261ec35129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260128} {:answer/id #uuid "6db551f8-65db-52e2-b235-316483262543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260837} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf47be0-ac22-5555-b8d0-61e1c5a24929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261372} {:answer/id #uuid "c9287898-574f-5d32-b2a6-21c00c5beef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261654} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c51b28-3d29-5999-b9fb-c6b01250d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260128} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fbbefbd-abc3-58c4-bb2e-77ec28ea9e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9184553-8293-59d4-95a8-2cc25c169eab" :question/math-academy-id "q-127198" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61102a14-b533-5513-a723-c254c2d06f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260709} {:answer/id #uuid "786a7883-0cb6-590c-8f39-483e7b2f4ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260927} {:answer/id #uuid "917128b1-1f2a-5c09-b649-63ccf0fa63f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261139} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4f88ee-8685-5a3d-b096-fe5bfea05166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261247} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd22cf0-a19c-5a79-a9fa-e7478861a8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261247} :answer-field/id #uuid "34367921-de6d-539e-8aa1-920b58ce0e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2de65327-4951-549c-b9fb-03c2c7cc11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127200" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f5177e-6bf6-5a90-909c-5c40f83cd489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260782} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5b7209-20d7-55b0-99b9-dc9f4fc2af0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261099} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc02a0a-836e-58f2-9074-006f830abe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261527} {:answer/id #uuid "dd252ef2-e9bb-5a70-8099-f8ffbfed03d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261830} {:answer/id #uuid "fea486a1-e02a-50be-9661-d1979f5e7aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2eae53-5bcf-5b7f-af5d-eafcc9d581c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b49bddc7-bed1-5bef-8ddb-d1f67820d61a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127207" :question/problem "![](images/66/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180fef8e-8ea9-590e-9071-b52b4ff39f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{62}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260084} {:answer/id #uuid "26d80b3f-6ec0-5698-94d6-f4acd16ceac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260215} {:answer/id #uuid "86077a7b-20d4-5ba1-ac9f-2d5a7c49bd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261039} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5c1838-c93e-5dfd-a8b4-ee4f539e435b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261110} {:answer/id #uuid "c792e5c1-7724-54f5-bafa-ba70e9aeab23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261641} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd7393b-e016-5b2f-a010-b9d7e2bb709b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f81e690-5559-586b-a65b-10e4fedf8848" :question/math-academy-id "q-127866" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(5,0,5),$ $B(6,-1,4),$ $C(9,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(6i-j+4k)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(9i+j)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =4i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =6i+j+5k \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7e2ae-f229-5350-bfe1-691c383da5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259879} {:answer/id #uuid "4884595d-d50d-5093-a9bb-f114519d1405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260502} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa82e9-537b-5d8c-9b2e-dd7bafe5cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261162} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a6eb21-de86-596c-8e62-5108683c8e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261611} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6f234a-fdc5-5088-b8f4-93467b60aada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261162} :answer-field/id #uuid "38599921-2cac-5ba2-a213-10d43c1e348b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1c91586-572d-5c90-b62f-69027efa2214" :question/math-academy-id "q-127908" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR,$ if $P(2,6,6),$ $Q(4,8,2),$ $R(0,5,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(4i+8j+2k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(5j+6k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))j+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "622785b6-0e5a-504c-8ba7-61c896b4597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260722} {:answer/id #uuid "679872d9-fa42-5cab-a5a3-e29f317c39aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260780} {:answer/id #uuid "8c028d49-b914-543f-9314-9b33da7f6f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261098} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ddb50b-ce6a-5a57-9ddd-5fad6c4d0a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261586} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0039be-579d-518d-a9d0-03877c66d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261098} :answer-field/id #uuid "8005319e-8e8b-5ab9-a565-7a8f41f83817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef5f09ae-6eb9-5c04-9bb6-e694acc7855a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127913" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR,$ if $P(6,1,5),$ $Q(5,1,7),$ $R(4,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(5i+j+7k)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(4i+2j)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i-9j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391e4ba1-cf5b-5a61-85aa-0b17144eae39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260382} {:answer/id #uuid "668cda2c-92ee-5199-b862-983b8a4c4afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260768} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2dfdd4-a52e-57e2-9f5e-3ab0a20c414b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260822} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb8fe9f-e32c-520a-816f-56dd8fde1270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260832} {:answer/id #uuid "8671701c-63a1-5620-a70a-e58d9e0297c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260768} :answer-field/id #uuid "8368d079-5a01-5ee1-9848-effbe38c3053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ecbe45d-cebe-5d0b-b79b-4337a04e8ef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127915" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,WXY,$ if $W(3,9,4),$ $X(1,7,3),$ $Y(6,12,8).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =x-w \\\\  & =(i+7j+3k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(6i+12j+8k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+4k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))i-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))j+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-5i+5j \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033166a8-eb6e-5026-9379-74758cf8f63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{33}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259903} {:answer/id #uuid "46f34cae-0d7f-57af-bd21-c29c4d58eb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260491} {:answer/id #uuid "86df7a60-c683-5984-ab1c-7b2102dfbe16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261051} {:answer/id #uuid "8941d219-3334-51f2-895b-7a537acbf651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261070} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fd4792-88e8-5bab-8423-291a5ffe487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261661} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af9ae3c-d3ff-5c29-96c7-03340f576f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3898211-8ec0-5bec-b86d-5fc41284c16c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128006" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(4,-1,1),$ $B(0,-3,4),$ $C(2,1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)i-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-12i+6j-12k \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17263848-7dad-541b-ad58-74745572f296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{38}}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260076} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb5bcd-7bf0-53b0-a60b-2f17c4f73037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260133} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0f2dce-a930-5b09-bde7-49e546203c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260526} {:answer/id #uuid "803e61e6-6963-5b24-9fb8-5a3e08371ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260987} {:answer/id #uuid "8d500b7a-085a-5399-943c-18364bfccf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8984f2f-6da2-54bf-9096-9f18e7aa0c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dbbc0374-2f40-56ea-b57e-f5227b194e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-128008" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of the $△\\,PRQ,$ if $P(3,0,4),$ $Q(2,3,1),$ $R(0,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29485b5b-c665-5779-a34e-0364eb6fa1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260238} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd34242-8e9e-5678-97c9-7cc01e6d4b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260525} {:answer/id #uuid "80b164c6-bba8-504a-bd8b-ab45cf5b2c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260996} {:answer/id #uuid "83f99092-f762-5530-8c3c-6fee86481012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261022} {:answer/id #uuid "a02e3ad1-637a-5291-aabd-24871e6db04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261265} :answer-field/id #uuid "658ccd8b-267b-53ca-9ec1-baa7bb2e0bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acfa5ee8-797a-57cb-8b1c-947581dc04d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-128013" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN,$ if $L(-1,4,1),$ $M(0,3,4),$ $N(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3j+4k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =2i+k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-i+5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1682dd1e-6a3e-585a-b8de-5b23b7ea94c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260072} {:answer/id #uuid "1689a068-b51c-5ee0-8061-d29ddb153fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260073} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2470ac-dd54-5b59-9478-94b73932f435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260124} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4bbf76-c7a5-5145-83ec-650fb6db1e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260964} {:answer/id #uuid "de38351b-baf5-5a90-a6e5-ee73c0a46168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261839} :answer-field/id #uuid "b65a4ca0-f974-538c-8c68-2ae22626c269" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94e0f9cc-6b5b-54e5-ac6a-648684ded023" :question/math-academy-id "q-128309" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(4-0)-1(1-0)-1(-3-20) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-23) \\\\  & =-8-1-23 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938435d8-69e4-5177-83be-c2661b220df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437788} {:answer/id #uuid "b0acf52f-1926-563b-90fe-5bd5c838a2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437788} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef069a99-e51d-5c41-b3dd-fbe618b3ae29" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fda1810e-40d9-5b5a-8ed9-7de0e91b6696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437790} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8badb5-ccf3-54b7-bf14-15f19c3c783e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437790} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6baa2c-bfa2-5841-829c-446168fc4d31" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd3030ea-f56b-54f2-b19e-0fe96063ba8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437792} {:answer/id #uuid "13768b60-6037-5979-87a7-85e46aa637f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437792} :answer-field/id #uuid "10609156-1bc3-5176-82f7-7da72d8cac5c" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45f9eaa1-ec03-587f-9925-ac1e03ddc805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667930} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeca510-070e-5b7e-a5b0-fde585d5adcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186667931} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb5d4cf-1c76-560f-9b77-bb0950007541" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f86fe2f1-042a-5183-8a18-518a658cc1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128474" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(9-5)-1(6-3)-1(10-9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9c8a655-dc30-5747-b5e0-efd4fa464943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437794} {:answer/id #uuid "b3238a25-1600-54c9-a92e-d6b1f31166ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437794} :answer-field/id #uuid "0457a549-1799-5124-a11e-6cbe8bf02f40" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86770289-d595-5a74-8241-7f7eaf943501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437796} {:answer/id #uuid "0065df67-0fde-5ae4-ab1b-04075255afaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437797} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c446f0-c232-5d1a-a9fa-08c4d973bbe4" :answer-field/key "$T$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d221ff-7342-5189-8083-2e45e2305d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437798} {:answer/id #uuid "db168e14-a68a-5486-95d1-8d4686854e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437799} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5f6d45-4846-5d6c-b409-f20f073263eb" :answer-field/key "$det(T)=-30$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "898815e0-65a8-55f9-b81f-a64efbb5e7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-128476" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(T)=-30$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96a78a3a-a96f-5296-bb6f-17bd0c2c1029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437812} {:answer/id #uuid "8a41bcde-2807-5a96-8245-df7493039a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437813} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d87a851-8d26-5425-83e8-dba94be07b21" :answer-field/key "$R$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d06dd62-d83d-54e9-b4ea-1974429d75ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437814} {:answer/id #uuid "423c9da1-fb5c-5eb1-aac0-e8f7d5033fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437815} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0097cd-c1c7-5438-b4af-b5844d0cf50a" :answer-field/key "$det(R)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2b6e65-193a-5e1a-ae4e-babcf8cd0b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437816} {:answer/id #uuid "4daf1704-b4bb-5190-aede-54368b9e49c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437816} :answer-field/id #uuid "727b8a81-c497-5e39-8d74-82d5d2b34ab9" :answer-field/key "$det(R)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0d8a3d8-8c1e-55f4-b495-022734d0f802" :question/math-academy-id "q-128484" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$R$ is a singular matrix” and $det(R)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fb98df-401c-5fa9-9f2f-08261df60aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259918} {:answer/id #uuid "220b8b83-724a-5cbe-853b-6caa42638775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260170} {:answer/id #uuid "726ac3fa-740a-5e90-81fa-f599fc3f37a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260875} {:answer/id #uuid "81ca9339-6bce-5219-8fae-787545463a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261005} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6b36bf-bffd-5918-ae53-d958ad6e391a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261005} :answer-field/id #uuid "c45caafc-858f-500e-9ef9-058205b847f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb6a7e0a-d356-5b58-a703-e0864f9a1a58" :question/math-academy-id "q-128500" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ y & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ y & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1-y)-5\\cdot (2-2y) \\\\  & =12y-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $12,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-12 & =12 \\\\ 12y & =24 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183c086d-012b-5f39-b92e-4550955dfcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260085} {:answer/id #uuid "2044861f-98f6-51d9-9cf5-e5080be450a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260152} {:answer/id #uuid "21a46985-36f6-5f1d-b213-16f3935ec5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260165} {:answer/id #uuid "91c5f6e6-19cd-5455-92f6-06f29a9d6687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261142} {:answer/id #uuid "c0800f44-af37-5d5d-b586-92ebc2148392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261575} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a39ba2f-181c-558b-b3c6-6fdf22cdca96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9517ddd6-18dd-5bea-948b-a75416c96cbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-128502" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}q & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & q \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (q+25)-3\\cdot (-1)+5 \\\\  & =2q+58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $28,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q+58 & =28 \\\\ 2q & =-30 \\\\ q & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe7a32f-bd64-5c3a-ac51-aa8e847ab23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439495} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b19982-26ea-52cf-bfb6-891798ebe687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439495} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9b26fe-a648-553a-a416-e7a5675ab1fe" :answer-field/key "$(-1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8879c732-ca2d-5143-a4fe-4257993d10ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439497} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d1f38f-73c6-5480-a2d4-f3c457b36e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439498} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ac6491-2fe7-5147-88bb-c17f0c42377d" :answer-field/key "$(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a47546-8467-57ce-8aa2-a8af7a03a45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439499} {:answer/id #uuid "9a881647-dacf-5b22-a11c-6dbd54eab45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439500} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b81800-b1bd-502a-8929-18d2b7af4a18" :answer-field/key "$(1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3587a5-2b35-5b4d-8516-1a3e694d12a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439501} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a10786-54a3-53dd-acf2-86b3f03692c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439501} :answer-field/id #uuid "0962b3c3-8729-55fc-a737-e4fe61f7b385" :answer-field/key "$(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48261b12-c86c-55b1-9e2a-f38acbfa450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128517" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1fe3ce-e39c-5f48-af02-f843ac8fe12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260280} {:answer/id #uuid "45a436bb-d9d3-5ae2-86cd-61af704fb4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260482} {:answer/id #uuid "8143f91a-8754-5cf9-b946-da41a6f966ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(y(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+x(1+2{y}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261002} {:answer/id #uuid "8b667d91-bb69-5253-9d69-5052dedca4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(x(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+xy(1-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261091} {:answer/id #uuid "c13fe910-b536-5489-9442-be8cb1a3b3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({x}^{2}(1+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+2{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260280} :answer-field/id #uuid "7821d5ef-cff7-57a3-8006-a2f8c44afc3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "76e2d854-0d75-5d56-8dc4-994bd31ca49e" :question/math-academy-id "q-128521" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $g(x,y)={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f78169-2f44-5c43-87d6-b815be978a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j-2xy\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260207} {:answer/id #uuid "296e6ebc-12ce-58be-b45b-a814a6ab10d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\,i+3xy\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260240} {:answer/id #uuid "38efeb4c-9bd1-5acf-ab43-dafd337eae24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\,i-2xz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260381} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf61748-52a6-5ede-9896-0da38336b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261530} {:answer/id #uuid "ea736859-5ca3-578a-b099-b7d2e3d293b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-2yz\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a18160-dc2f-533e-9be0-793491ff920c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13103db1-962a-564a-a73e-3a0e51d17668" :question/math-academy-id "q-128529" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\, &  & ∇f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{1}{xyz},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y,z)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)∇h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k)+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,i+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,j+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\,k-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i+2{z}^{2}\\,j+3yz\\,k-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-yz\\,k \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e5b4ed-eeb6-50fd-9d7c-a2cadad2150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263092} {:answer/id #uuid "17975e13-48f0-5e64-b97d-6b4b3abe3bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263223} {:answer/id #uuid "44bfef5a-564c-5524-bcfb-24152afe7765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263603} {:answer/id #uuid "65042cb7-a615-5767-8903-e2b88dbd8cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263874} {:answer/id #uuid "92181a3e-9464-53f8-8d0a-4cee36eefaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263874} :answer-field/id #uuid "3963b998-8449-5414-8913-164290af0fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fa0e8d7-3133-5471-9c08-501132fe188f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129322" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c1cdaa-8ccc-5f2d-a004-9ae4846bab35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264272} {:answer/id #uuid "aa551ce0-e36c-55d8-b6d7-caa9fba4882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264454} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa9a350-3a47-50f2-8f0e-6a5936d8b19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264818} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8fac4d-b063-52b0-8ed7-a78b532ee43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265043} {:answer/id #uuid "fccf2baa-e984-5f4c-9fc3-9a03dfb968c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265219}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264454} :answer-field/id #uuid "619cb15a-2c93-5319-81cd-c8a7665c35b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d53fcb5b-03e0-54f4-94bd-b9eac7281e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-129329" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ad1bd2-8442-5dfe-b6b9-a6a220f4b699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263602} {:answer/id #uuid "73354caa-11ab-5a26-90fd-714c92eefd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264000} {:answer/id #uuid "a62e5c28-ad0a-52bf-bd5e-b5aca29871b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264421} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d81618-b514-541a-badf-cb234ce0914d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264435} {:answer/id #uuid "ab554095-56b3-5440-8583-dc412455d74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264435} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f223cdc-4ab9-5d2a-9c8f-fc56257df30b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d67ac27-26e1-5580-9eb2-034565720f25" :question/math-academy-id "q-129332" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd3a569-22f5-58d9-b2f8-184a33be9932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263543} {:answer/id #uuid "73948bda-635e-5d60-afcf-95c733c7ad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264002} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8ab39b-0d20-5e12-be94-5fb9aa528858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264617} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e3ae72-b88f-5f06-b306-aaafce164c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264726} {:answer/id #uuid "dc64dec8-2f23-5153-a3b2-2728e3383078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264002} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1300cc-2bf9-55dc-b250-0c25b95b165f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "715f6fef-c679-5b35-a9f5-f8ac13307bba" :question/math-academy-id "q-129335" :question/problem "Determine $$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}-3({2}^{2})(2)+3(2)({2}^{2})-{2}^{3}}{{2}^{2}-2(2)(2)+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-24+24-8}{4-8+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)(x-y)^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y) & =2-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0569550f-51bd-575d-b80b-ec093b07682c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263049} {:answer/id #uuid "298d83f3-e1e3-5bce-96c8-4b0b8973f332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263357} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0c56eb-cdff-5db9-9329-b25d29d16a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264610} {:answer/id #uuid "ba22f1e6-506d-59b4-8ad2-edde9f213b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264611} {:answer/id #uuid "e27a6e9e-7126-5be8-b5d1-8378b185a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264967} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66c8923-8d97-5e59-b5ab-27bce45dda8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa92e0c7-08cd-5cb2-af58-76718486c28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129339" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}}{x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b2e738-d4ff-5a73-bf88-ee96a546601e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263713} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5d18cd-26e9-513a-9ac2-d9e28cdc3a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263957} {:answer/id #uuid "859fba6d-65df-52f9-8934-78218673bfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264151} {:answer/id #uuid "b3999021-6e0d-58f6-8af2-534ecd301f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264540} {:answer/id #uuid "f16bc0b6-a366-5c5f-a505-fcaf77e8d63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265120} :answer-field/id #uuid "828835a1-d776-5ae6-8933-2aafe083fed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8bc5519-0c3a-5438-9140-d93c11fd7b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-129342" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0baa5e10-5459-5732-9dd7-5d51a31a7b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263111} {:answer/id #uuid "3fec9ee5-6ae2-58fe-8a38-325b070dba28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263564} {:answer/id #uuid "815895b6-8daa-5f01-b1e0-50eb24942d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264112} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0f99ee-d7bb-5f69-b6bb-53b9408bee54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265068} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd17ed5-cd78-5b95-8110-e650724142fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f643f621-c0dd-5dc1-b633-084448f646c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43a8fc7d-552c-5ee1-ac4a-431ddf37f045" :question/math-academy-id "q-129345" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}-1}{({1}^{2})(2+(-1))-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{(x\\sqrt{y+z}-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)}(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36de98d3-7bb5-5915-95e1-b5b78a147e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263490} {:answer/id #uuid "41df6099-6368-5629-97e7-c9681245feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263583} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fbcaf4-6b4a-5eed-b561-6f65f5756d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264452} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ae1d76-9dd1-56fd-88c0-3619b135a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264554} {:answer/id #uuid "e08b40a4-9f01-5372-973d-b6907da00dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264949} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df59ef6-6f84-55da-879a-62c922b8039c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "98eb7cb6-6c4e-52ce-9f7d-3ac96c9eabeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129346" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b35a9a-4cdd-5c91-90ee-71d656ed495e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263007} {:answer/id #uuid "528d1499-5272-5dd7-b3fb-6258a07d6726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263712} {:answer/id #uuid "a015c9d1-4b16-5124-b907-8539b7f1f1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264363} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d18dad-121a-5539-aa6a-64f5b013d4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264750} {:answer/id #uuid "fde472b2-1fec-5b3a-b7d9-d562106cbe12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264750} :answer-field/id #uuid "a416a2ef-a857-581b-bbef-47029f3cce4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b38eaadb-c5b0-5008-b859-4e13e1a65105" :question/math-academy-id "q-129348" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}-3}{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9-9}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}]}
